## An analytic mechanism for a potential rigorous upper tail

This ordinary block bound reduces the missing analytic work to explicit rotation spacing. Put alpha=1/(2pi), y_n=||n alpha-1/4||. Since1-cos(2pi y)>=8y^2 for |y|<=1/2 and1-u<=exp(-u),

    a_n <= exp(-(8/3)*n*y_n^2)/n.

For any q>=2 let delta_q=min(1<=h<q)||h alpha||>0. In q consecutive phases, distinct points are separated on the circle by at least delta_q. Thus the count of points within distance t<=1/2 from1/4 is <=1+2t/delta_q. Integrating the derivative of the decreasing Gaussian against this counting function gives, for X>0,

    sum_over_one_q_block exp(-(8/3)*X*y_n^2)
       <= 1+sqrt(3pi/8)/(delta_q*sqrt X).

A leftover shorter block obeys the same bound. Partitioning integer n in[X,2X) into ceil(X/q) blocks (integer X) consequently gives the rigorous dyadic estimate

    sum(X<=n<2X) a_n
      <= ceil(X/q)/X * (1+sqrt(3pi/8)/(delta_q*sqrt X)).

No mixing or independence assumption is used. Exact continued-fraction/interval bounds could give good delta_q at each scale, then an explicit irrationality-measure bound could handle the remote tail. Boppana's globally explicit exponent20 is available as a cited dependency; I have not completed the constants or the multiscale summation necessary for a narrow enclosure. This bound is classical packing plus a Gaussian envelope, not a separate discovery.

